9. Daemon Thread

Daemon threads are background threads that support user threads.

Examples:

Garbage Collector (GC)
Reference Handler
Common Cleaner

A daemon thread automatically terminates when all user (non-daemon) threads have finished.

Example:

public static void main(String[] args) {

    Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {

        while (true) {
            System.out.println(1);
        }

    });

    t1.setDaemon(true);   // Must be called before start()

    t1.start();

    return;
}

Here:

t1 runs continuously.
Since it is a daemon thread, when the main thread (and all other user threads) finish, the JVM exits and t1 is stopped automatically.

Example:

If the main thread sleeps for 2 seconds,

Thread.sleep(2000);

the daemon thread keeps running during those 2 seconds.

When the main thread exits, the daemon thread also terminates automatically.

